From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: aiueo52 <aiueo52@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 3 Sep 2026 20:56:04 +0900
Subject: [PATCH 035/105] hc: reshape the fused combine around per-branch
 column slices

The first cut sliced the flat HC*HS row, which made two things go wrong. A slice
straddled branch boundaries, so `block_output` had to be indexed by `offs % HS` --
an expression Triton cannot prove contiguous, so every access in the kernel fell
back to scalar `ld.global.b16`. And the gate had to be selected per element with a
`tl.where` chain instead of once per branch.

Program (m, q) now owns the same SUB-wide column window in all HC branches, with SUB
a power of two dividing HS. That makes the working set one [HC, SUB] tile whose rows
are contiguous and 16 B aligned (checked by compiling to PTX for a sm_120 target with
the divisibility attributes the JIT supplies: every access is now ld/st.global.v4.b32),
lets `block_output` and the partials be read once per program, and turns the gate into
an [HC] vector from a single vectorised partials load.

Traffic per 16-row call drops with it: the normed row and the block output are now
read exactly once each (~2.4 MB total vs ~3.6 MB for the CUDA pair, which re-reads
the normed row per branch in the gate kernel and the block output per branch in the
apply kernel).

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>
